// @error a `type` declaration may not take the built-in type name `f64`
// @error a `type` declaration may not take the built-in type name `i64`
// @error a `type` declaration may not take the built-in type name `string`
// D1585 — THE THREE POSITIONS OF ONE RULE, in one program: a dependency's EXPORTED `type`,
// a dependency's PRIVATE `type`, and the ENTRY module's own. All three ran before this row,
// and the entry's is the one that shows the dodge was never about the module BOUNDARY: a
// single-file `type string` is refused, and the same declaration in the same entry file is
// not, once an `import` puts the program into module mode and `modRwStmt` renames it to
// `string$m0` before `checkProgram`'s screen ever sees it.
//
// BOTH DECLARATION NODES are covered — the parser splits a struct alias (`TypeDecl`) from a
// union alias (`UnionDecl`), and `f64` here is the first while `i64` is the second.
import { ping } from "./lib"

type string = { s: i32 }

print(ping())
